What is the definition of a blended family, and how does it relate to other family structures?
A blended family, also known as a stepfamily, is a family unit that consists of a married couple with children from a previous relationship. This type of family is formed when two people with children from a previous marriage or relationship get married and have a child together. The term "blended" is used to describe this type of family because it combines the children from both partners' previous relationships into a single family unit.

In a blended family, the children from the previous relationships are considered step-siblings to one another, and any subsequent child born to the couple is a half-sibling of the respective members' prior children. This type of family structure is also sometimes referred to as a "complex" family, as it involves a combination of biological and step-relationships.

Blended families can be further categorized into different types, such as neotraditional, matriarchal, and romantic, each with their own characteristics and dynamics. Neotraditional blended families, for example, are those in which both parents share the responsibility of the children, while matriarchal blended families are those in which the mother plays a dominant role.
