What is the FM dial and how does it work?
The FM dial, also known as the frequency modulation dial, is a control on a radio that allows users to tune into different FM radio stations. It works by adjusting the frequency of the radio's receiver to match the frequency of the FM broadcast signal being transmitted by the radio station.

In more technical terms, FM radio uses frequency modulation to encode audio signals onto a carrier wave. The FM dial allows the user to select a specific frequency within the FM broadcast band (typically between 88 MHz and 108 MHz) to receive the desired station's signal. When the dial is turned, it changes the local oscillator frequency of the radio's receiver, allowing it to lock onto the frequency of the selected station and decode the audio signal.
