The historical context of the drinking age in Massachusetts leading up to the change to 21 is as follows:

Prior to 1984, the drinking age in Massachusetts was 20. However, in response to the National Minimum Drinking Age Act of 1984, which tied federal highway funds to states maintaining a minimum drinking age of 21, Governor Michael S. Dukakis signed a bill on December 4, 1984, raising the drinking age in Massachusetts from 20 to 21. This change was made to avoid a reduction in federal highway funding.
