Analyze the contributions of ancient Greek philosophers, including Aristotle and Epicurus, to the development of the scientific method.
Aristotle introduced empiricism and the notion that universal truths can be arrived at via observation. He also formulated a scientific method, as seen in his work "Posterior Analytics", which included a demonstration method. Additionally, Aristotle reconciled abstract thought with observation, although he did not accept that knowledge acquired by induction could be counted as scientific knowledge.

Epicurus, on the other hand, expanded on the idea of atoms, which was first proposed by Democritus. This concept of atoms laid the foundation for later scientific inquiry.

In summary, the ancient Greek philosophers, particularly Aristotle and Epicurus, made significant contributions to the development of the scientific method by introducing empiricism, observation, and the concept of atoms.
