Describe the specific dates and years when daylight saving time was first introduced in Australia, and how it has been modified or adjusted over time, including the initial dates of implementation for each state and territory.
Daylight saving time was first introduced in Australia during World War I. The initial dates of implementation for each state and territory were:

*   All states and territories observed daylight saving during World War I and World War II.
*   In 1968, Tasmania became the first state since the war to practise daylight saving.
*   In 1971, New South Wales, Victoria, Queensland, South Australia, and the Australian Capital Territory followed Tasmania by observing daylight saving.
*   Western Australia and the Northern Territory did not observe daylight saving at that time.
*   Queensland abandoned daylight saving time in 1972.

The dates of implementation for each state and territory have been modified or adjusted over time. For example:

*   In 2000, all of the eastern jurisdictions that normally observe DST – New South Wales, Victoria, the ACT, and Tasmania – began DST early because of the Summer Olympic Games and the Summer Paralympic Games held in Sydney. These jurisdictions moved to DST on 27 August 2000. South Australians did not change their clocks until the usual date, which was 29 October 2000.
*   In 2006, all of the states that followed DST (the above states and South Australia) delayed their return to Standard Times by one week, because of the 2006 Commonwealth Games in Melbourne. Daylight-saving time ended on 2 April 2006.
*   From 2008/09, daylight saving has been extended another four weeks
