Explain the Universal Declaration of Human Rights (UDHR) and its significance in ensuring the right to family.
The Universal Declaration of Human Rights (UDHR) is a foundational document adopted by the United Nations General Assembly in 1948. It sets out a broad range of fundamental human rights and freedoms to be universally protected and respected. Specifically, the UDHR ensures the right to family by stating that:

*   Article 16: "Men and women of full age, without any limitation due to race, nationality or religion, have the right to marry and to found a family. They are entitled to equal rights as to marriage, during marriage and at its dissolution. In the case of dissolution, both spouses shall have the right to civil effects resulting from the marriage, in community property shall be shared between them."
*   Article 10: "Everyone is entitled in full equality to a fair and public hearing by an independent and impartial tribunal, in the determination of his rights and obligations and of any criminal charge against him."

The UDHR's significance in ensuring the right to family lies in its recognition of the importance of family as a fundamental unit of society. It emphasizes the need for equal rights and responsibilities between spouses, as well as the protection of the family unit from discrimination and violence. The UDHR's principles have been influential in shaping international human rights law and have been incorporated into various national laws and policies around the world.
