What is the historical context surrounding the term 'Great Depression'?
The term "Great Depression" has been used to refer to economic downturns in the past, dating back to the 19th century. The first major American economic crisis, the Panic of 1819, was described as a "depression" by President James Monroe. The term was also used to describe the Depression of 1920-21, referred to as a "depression" by President Calvin Coolidge. However, the term gained widespread use to describe the global economic downturn of the 1930s, which was formally coined by British economist Lionel Robbins in his 1934 book "The Great Depression". US President Herbert Hoover is also credited with popularizing the term, informally referring to the downturn.
