Identify the origin and evolution of the phrase 'When in Rome, do as the Romans' to understand its historical context and possible influences.
The phrase "When in Rome, do as the Romans do" originated from a proverb attributed to Saint Ambrose, a 4th-century Christian bishop. The phrase is derived from a quote by Ambrose, where he advised Saint Monica and her son, Saint Augustine, to follow the customs of the local church where they were residing. The original quote is:

"When I am at Rome, I fast on a Saturday; when I am at Milan, I do not. Follow the custom of the church where you are."

Over time, the phrase evolved into "When in Rome, do as the Romans do," which means that it is advisable to follow the conventions of the area in which you are residing or visiting. This phrase has been used to convey the idea of adapting to local customs and traditions when in a new place.

The phrase gained popularity and was later modified to "When in Rome, do as the Pope does," which is a play on the original phrase. The phrase has also been used in a humorous way, as seen in the title of the album "When in Rome Do as The Vandals," which is a play on the original phrase and references the Vandals, a Germanic tribe that invaded the Roman Empire.

The historical context of the phrase is rooted in the early Christian church, where Saint Ambrose's advice was meant to promote unity and respect for local customs. The phrase has since been used in various contexts to convey the idea of adapting to new environments and respecting local traditions
